 # Hybrid Ray-Casting
 
-Also called: Intermediate Image Ray Casting, Template-based Raycasting, Warping Ray Casting
+Also called: Intermediate Image Ray Casting, Warping Ray Casting
 
 ## Overview
-Hybrid Ray-Casting is a volume rendering technique that generates an intermediate image aligned with one of the volume's principal axes (faces) and then warps this image onto the final view plane [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of irregular memory access patterns inherent in standard image-order ray casting by ensuring that voxels are read in regular, object-order slices parallel to the base plane [S1, S2]. Unlike [Volume Ray Casting](3161-volume-ray-casting.md), which resamples the volume directly in view space, Hybrid Ray-Casting decouples the volume traversal from the final projection, enabling high-performance implementations on parallel architectures [S1, S2].
+Hybrid Ray-Casting is a volume rendering technique that combines object-order data access with image-order final projection by rendering an intermediate image aligned with a volume face and warping it to the view plane [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of irregular memory access patterns in standard image-order ray casting by ensuring voxels are read in contiguous slices, thereby enabling high-performance implementations on parallel architectures [S1, S2]. Unlike [Volume Ray Casting](3161-volume-ray-casting.md), which resamples the volume directly along view rays, this method decouples the volume traversal from the final viewpoint, using a shear-warp transformation to maintain a one-to-one mapping of ray samples onto the volume grid [S2].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** Volume dataset $V$ (typically a 3D grid of voxels), camera parameters (view direction, projection type), and base plane orientation [S1, S2].
-**Outputs:** Final rendered image on the image plane [S1, S2].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** A 3D volume dataset $V$ defined on a regular grid; a camera view defined by the image plane and view direction.
+*   **Output:** A 2D image on the final image plane.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-1. **Volume Data:** A 3D array of voxel values [G].
-2. **Slice Access:** The algorithm assumes access to slices (2D planes) of this data [S1].
-3. **Intermediate Image Buffer:** A 2D array storing the rendered pixels for the base plane [G].
-4. **Warping Parameters:** Affine transformation matrices (shear, scale) derived from the camera view relative to the volume axes [S2].
+*   **Volume Data:** Stored as a 3D array of voxels. The memory layout is optimized for sequential access along planes parallel to the base plane.
+*   **Intermediate Image:** A 2D buffer storing the composited color and opacity for the base plane.
+*   **Warping Parameters:** Affine transformation matrices (shear and scale) defining the mapping from the base plane to the view plane.
 
-**Steps:**
-1. **Base Plane Selection:** Identify the volume face (slice direction) most parallel and nearest to the image plane [S1].
-2. **Volume Transformation (Shear/Scale):** Transform the volume into an intermediate coordinate system such that the view direction aligns with one of the principal axes (typically the z-axis of the base plane) [S2]. This transformation typically involves a shear and, for perspective projections, a scale of the original slices [S2]. The transformation into an intermediate coordinate system achieves a one-to-one mapping of ray-samples onto the volume [S2].
-3. **Ray Casting on Base Plane:** Send rays from each pixel on the base plane into the volume [S1]. Read and process voxels in planes (slices) parallel to the base plane [S1]. Within each slice, read voxels from memory a scanline at a time in top-to-bottom order [S1]. This provides regular, object-order data access [S1]. Accumulate color and opacity along the ray to produce the intermediate image [G].
-4. **Warping:** Apply a 2D geometric transformation (warp) to the intermediate image to map it onto the final view plane [S1, S2]. This step corrects for the angle between the base plane and the image plane [S1].
+**Method Steps:**
+1.  **Base Plane Selection:** Identify the face of the volume data that is most parallel and nearest to the image plane. Define a "base plane" co-planar to this face [S1].
+2.  **Volume Transformation (Shear/Scale):** Transform the volume into an intermediate coordinate system such that the resample locations for rays originating from the base plane coincide with integer grid points. This typically involves a shear transformation, and for perspective projections, a scale transformation of the original slices [S2].
+3.  **Base Plane Rendering:** Send rays from each pixel on the base plane into the volume. Because the base plane is parallel to a volume face, rays traverse the volume in planes (slices) parallel to the base plane.
+    *   Within each slice, voxels are read from memory a scanline at a time in top-to-bottom order [S1].
+    *   Perform volume integration (compositing) along each ray to produce the base-plane image [S1, S2].
+4.  **Warping:** Warp (2D transform) the resulting base-plane image onto the final image plane. This step accounts for the angle between the base plane and the view direction [S1, S2].
 
